    "review": "## Summary of Findings\n\nThe code change in `DefaultDriverService.delete` correctly addresses the identified bug by ensuring that the soft-delete flag (`setDeleted(true)`) is persisted to the database via a call to `driverRepository.save(driverDO)`. This resolves the issue where the deletion was only applied in memory and not saved.\n\nThe implementation adheres strictly to the goal\u2019s constraints:\n- The method signature remains unchanged.\n- No other methods in the class are modified.\n- The `@Transactional` annotation is preserved.\n- Only the necessary persistence logic (`driverRepository.save`) is added to fix the bug.\n\nNo style, architecture, or testing coverage issues were introduced by this change. It is a minimal and targeted fix that satisfies all stated requirements.\n\n## Code Review\n\n### Summary of Findings\n\nThe code change corrects a critical bug in `DefaultDriverService.delete()` by ensuring that the soft-delete flag is persisted to the database after being set in memory. This resolves an issue where the driver would still appear as active despite deletion, because the save operation was missing.\n\n### Merge Readiness\n\nThe code change fixes the reported bug and meets all specified constraints:\n- The method signature remains unchanged.\n- Only the required persistence logic (`driverRepository.save(driverDO)`) is added.\n- No other files or methods are modified.\n- No style, testing, or architectural issues were found.\n\n\u2705 **Approved**. This is a correct and minimal fix that resolves the identified issue without side effects.\n\n## How to Run the Application\n\nThe provided file `DefaultDriverService.java` is part of a Spring Boot application using standard Maven conventions. To compile and run the application:\n\n1. **Compile the project:**\n   ```bash\n   mvn clean compile\n   ```\n\n2. **Run the application (e.g., using Spring Boot plugin):**\n   ```bash\n   mvn spring-boot:run\n   ```\n\n3. **Verify functionality:**\n   - Ensure that calling `DefaultDriverService.delete(Long driverId)` now properly persists the soft-delete flag.\n   - Confirm that after deletion, the driver is no longer returned by queries that filter out deleted records.\n\n*Note:* The exact REST endpoints or specific behavior of `DriverController` is not provided in this review context, so the precise HTTP calls to trigger `delete()` are not determinable from the supplied evidence. However, standard Spring Boot practices apply for invoking service methods through controllers or direct test cases.",
